---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Scripture, Theology and Christian life in the modern world</h1>
			<p>
				Everyone becomes a confused catholic at one point or another. In 2025, after decades away from the Church,
				I returned. Before returning, I did what many of us do and began to look stuff up on the internet
				<strong>-- what a time to be alive!</strong>
			</p>
			<p>

				At that point, I typed in <code>confused catholic</code> into a search engine. The results were mainly some letter
				I'd never heard of.
			</p>
			<p>
				As time went on, I learned that this letter was a little more important and upsetting than
				I had initially gleaned. It's called: <a href="https://www.fisheaters.com/srpdf/anopenlettertoconfusedcatholics.pdf">
				"An open letter to confused Catholics."</a> Its author is none other than deceased SSPX Bishop, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcel_Lefebvre">
				Marcel Lefebvre</a> This man is the architect of the schismatic traditionalism we see in Catholicism today.
			</p>
			<p>
				As writer, I occasionally check SEO (search engine optimization) rankings for certain terms. For an entire year,
				Lefebvre's letter still continued to show up at the number one rank. I find this highly troubling. I've already been
				writing about my faith for quite some time -- and I write technical, SEO content and thought leadership by trade.

				The goal of this site is pretty simple.
				
				<strong>When a lapsed and confused Catholic explores their faith online, I want them to find something positive.
					We don't need more Lefebvre, he's done enough damage. We need resources.
				</strong>
			</p>
			<p>
				Protestants talk about Sola Scriptura, that Scripture alone is the only authority in religion. Personally,
				I don't believe in Sola Scriptura -- I am a Catholic after all. However, I do believe that Aperta Scriptura
				(open Scripture) -- is something the world desperately needs.
			</p>
			
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
